1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,811
This is KubeSage, an AI SRE team for Kubernetes.

2
00:00:03,811 --> 00:00:07,010
Here is how it works, in under three minutes.

3
00:00:08,413 --> 00:00:09,692
First, the summary.

4
00:00:09,692 --> 00:00:15,613
KubeSage finds capacity you pay for but never use, and right-sizes it within guardrails.

5
00:00:15,613 --> 00:00:22,140
When something breaks, a team of AI agents gathers evidence, finds the cause, and writes the fix.

6
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:26,783
Agent fixes become git commits that a person reviews before applying.

7
00:00:28,187 --> 00:00:31,750
Picture a hypothetical online store on Azure Kubernetes.

8
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:37,032
Five services share one general-purpose node at two hundred eighty dollars a month.

9
00:00:37,032 --> 00:00:39,704
Some requests were set high, just in case:

10
00:00:39,704 --> 00:00:43,268
two services reserve two cores each and use almost none.

11
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:46,195
A cheaper compute-optimised node stands ready.

12
00:00:46,195 --> 00:00:49,567
Tonight, a ledger cache ships with too little memory.

13
00:00:50,982 --> 00:00:54,388
To find waste, KubeSage reads thirty days of usage.

14
00:00:54,388 --> 00:00:58,997
Statistics are free, and healthy workloads stop after a quick triage.

15
00:00:58,997 --> 00:01:01,868
Only harder cases get more model reasoning.

16
00:01:01,868 --> 00:01:06,544
Guardrails floor requests from history and keep scaling within bounds.

17
00:01:06,544 --> 00:01:10,952
The result is a plan for requests, autoscaling, and the node pool.

18
00:01:12,357 --> 00:01:16,733
When an alert fires, four small agents gather evidence in parallel:

19
00:01:16,733 --> 00:01:19,868
metrics, system resources, alerts, and security.

20
00:01:19,868 --> 00:01:26,594
A larger Thinker model reads their findings and your team's plain English rules to find the root cause.

21
00:01:26,594 --> 00:01:28,357
An Executor writes the fix.

22
00:01:28,357 --> 00:01:32,667
Policy checks limit each edit's scope and type, before the commit.

23
00:01:34,874 --> 00:01:38,880
Here is the KubeSage dashboard on that simulated cluster.

24
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:44,854
Each glass box is a pod, as tall as the CPU it reserves, filled as far as it is used.

25
00:01:45,557 --> 00:01:51,210
Analyze finds that a cheaper node would save about thirty-four dollars a month, twelve percent.

26
00:01:51,210 --> 00:01:54,007
The model calls to find it cost about one cent.

27
00:01:54,619 --> 00:01:58,226
Apply moves the right-sized services to the cheaper node.

28
00:01:58,226 --> 00:02:01,769
Report-worker scales to zero outside its nightly window.

29
00:02:02,652 --> 00:02:04,456
Now the bad release ships.

30
00:02:04,456 --> 00:02:08,825
Ledger-cache needs more memory than its limit, so it is killed.

31
00:02:08,825 --> 00:02:11,252
The ContainerOOMKilled alert fires.

32
00:02:11,870 --> 00:02:13,531
The team starts on its own.

33
00:02:13,531 --> 00:02:19,007
The four sub-agents report, the Thinker finds the cause, and the Executor writes the fix.

34
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:21,530
The chat on the left shows each hand-off.

35
00:02:22,148 --> 00:02:29,682
The commit raises ledger-cache's memory, proposes a memory-optimised node pool, and adds two security fixes.

36
00:02:29,682 --> 00:02:31,565
Sixteen seconds, two cents.

37
00:02:31,565 --> 00:02:33,518
A person decides what ships.

38
00:02:34,076 --> 00:02:35,106
That is KubeSage.

39
00:02:35,106 --> 00:02:39,646
It finds the waste, fixes what breaks, and leaves the decision to a person.
